#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3061cdb9-3f5e-578e-aa48-1b24ed90d2f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283598} {:answer/id #uuid "dd879f56-1774-57f5-bdfa-3ece34b0c42c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283599} {:answer/id #uuid "3259e6e4-8f4a-5a93-a58a-3f5ccd7b0794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283600} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc7dec1-6220-541d-b15c-f7806182ca52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283601} {:answer/id #uuid "092a7670-38a2-597e-8b3c-c65a3e952468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd879f56-1774-57f5-bdfa-3ece34b0c42c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283599} :answer-field/id #uuid "8233a48f-b290-5757-8915-04df2ffaa58e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56f56a99-ae3f-56b5-9e3f-612f5a2d8c37" :question/math-academy-id "q-18285" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-0+(0-2) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\cdot (-2)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "392b40d0-742a-5201-9772-387ceac68044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321375} {:answer/id #uuid "5fab8c5b-110a-5f53-b3c9-4fd77954da68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fab8c5b-110a-5f53-b3c9-4fd77954da68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321376} :answer-field/id #uuid "abc3316d-4bab-54f5-aa83-c84a92afd041" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f1605a7c-6610-56ff-a112-240e6e5bbd01" :question/math-academy-id "q-275757" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & -2\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2+k)-(-4+2)-(2k+2) \\\\  & =2+k+2-2k-2 \\\\  & =2-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}^{t}={c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-k}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{k-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{k-2} & =-1 \\\\ -1\\cdot (k-2) & =2 \\\\ k-2 & =-2 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9092226-c93b-564e-8674-90cdda0e56fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9092226-c93b-564e-8674-90cdda0e56fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321379} :answer-field/id #uuid "50ec604c-1d1b-5e6c-8052-8a4b3e24aec1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e46c518-3e5c-53ed-8495-f09dea2fdfbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-319409" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n${a}_{32}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(6-0)-0(-3-0)-1(1-0) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}^{t}={c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(2-0) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55b1616d-08df-5ad7-ba02-103428efcd35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55b1616d-08df-5ad7-ba02-103428efcd35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288564} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fb06bcc-74a6-5677-a4f1-bd2028bab163" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfe6bc14-6591-5f71-bb33-e7e4cf7f033e" :question/math-academy-id "q-319413" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n${a}_{12}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(6-0)-0(-2-0)-1(1-0) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0+1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfddfff-2efc-5935-a57b-be628caa2616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288567} {:answer/id #uuid "69b0dac9-0505-5959-b6b1-5693c223ec42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288568} {:answer/id #uuid "88892a86-3a54-5a28-8119-ff95f09f7e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288569} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd800ed-e50c-554d-9737-eeda0629a09b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288570} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed7a78d-bd89-5f9b-94b1-6f24d665fcd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88892a86-3a54-5a28-8119-ff95f09f7e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288569} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8b32d26-4ce1-5e64-80c3-4102cfcaad63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2b1695b-282d-54b6-9ac9-0c3ba20a588b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73542" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ k & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $1.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ k & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2-0)+(-2k-0)+0 \\\\  & =-2k-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(1-(-3)) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2k-2}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{k+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{32}^{′}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{k+1} & =1 \\\\ 2 & =k+1 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
